1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:07,421
Seven hundred and forty six drug shortage
entries on the FDA list were updated in a
single week.

2
00:00:07,421 --> 00:00:10,700
Which of them are on your hospital's shelf?

3
00:00:11,100 --> 00:00:15,134
That is the daily job of a hospital pharmacy
buyer.

4
00:00:15,134 --> 00:00:21,700
This is Shortage Radar, running right now,
in real time, against the real FDA data.

5
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:41,700
Before any model is involved, it has already
read the FDA feed through Zetaris, replayed
every saved snapshot in the World Model so
it knows what changed, joined the hospital's
stock with the FDA list in one query, and
screened every third party field with the
Scout guard.

6
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:46,872
Now Nemotron, through Nightshift, is
choosing what to cover.

7
00:00:46,872 --> 00:00:56,894
This is the slow step, because it is the one
place the model is allowed to think:
choosing what matters is the judgement call.

8
00:00:56,894 --> 00:01:09,700
After that it writes one item at a time with
thinking off, and a checker written in plain
code verifies every claim, so a slow model
cannot talk its way past it.

9
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,860
While that finishes, here is what is under
the hood.

10
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:20,968
Three Meterless engines do real work, and
each passes its official test: World Model,
eight of eight.

11
00:01:20,968 --> 00:01:22,939
H-MEM, thirteen of thirteen.

12
00:01:22,939 --> 00:01:28,147
Scout Intent, injection precision one point
zero, recall point nine eight.

13
00:01:28,147 --> 00:01:41,800
The limits are on the page too: the hospital
is fictional, Scout is pattern based, and
the agent never predicts a shortage, because
when we tested whether recalls predict them,
the data said no.

14
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:56,957
Six items need action today.

15
00:01:56,957 --> 00:02:07,700
Etomidate went from available to
unavailable, with six days of stock left,
and it names exactly what to buy instead:
the same strength, from another
manufacturer.

16
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:10,012
Every line has a receipt.

17
00:02:10,012 --> 00:02:24,700
The drawer shows the swap options, and below
that the evidence behind each sentence: the
stock, the FDA status history from the World
Model, and the Zetaris query with its SQL,
one click away.

18
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:27,830
A swap has to be the same strength.

19
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:36,254
A different concentration is a medication
error, so on Morphine the other products are
not offered as swaps.

20
00:02:36,254 --> 00:02:40,700
They go to a pharmacist for review, each
with its reason.

21
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:43,970
When the buyer decides, H-MEM remembers.

22
00:02:43,970 --> 00:02:48,921
I record that I bought the alternate, and
Etomidate moves to Handled.

23
00:02:48,921 --> 00:02:53,800
It stays out of tomorrow's brief until the
FDA status changes again.

24
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:55,626
Everything is logged.

25
00:02:55,626 --> 00:03:04,522
Each line names the agent, the Zetaris
query, and the model that answered: Nemotron
three point five Lightning, through
Nightshift.

26
00:03:04,522 --> 00:03:08,800
The planner thinks, the writer doesn't, and
the critic is code.

27
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:12,528
And anyone can run it with no keys at all.

28
00:03:12,528 --> 00:03:21,800
Sample mode replays a recorded live run,
labelled as a replay, so a judge sees
exactly what the live system produced.

29
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:25,036
Real data, real queries, real receipts.

30
00:03:25,036 --> 00:03:28,600
Shortage Radar doesn't guess what will run
short.

31
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:31,800
It tells you what to do about what just did.

